**Requirements (assumed).** Candidates create profiles and apply to jobs; recruiters post jobs and view applicants; search jobs by skill/location. Keep it to a core in 30 minutes.

**Entities.** `Person(id, name, email, skills)`, `Job(id, title, company, location, status)`, and the relationship **applies to**.

**Key modelling decision.** A person applies to many jobs and a job has many applicants, so it is many-to-many. A plain `@ManyToMany` with a join table `person_job(person_id, job_id)` works only if the link carries no data. A job application always does (applied date, status, resume version), so a strong answer promotes it to an entity `Application(id, person, job, appliedAt, status)` with `@ManyToOne` on both sides and a unique constraint on `(person_id, job_id)`. Say this explicitly, then show you can also write the plain mapping:

```java
@Entity class Person {
  @ManyToMany
  @JoinTable(name = "person_job",
    joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "person_id"),
    inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "job_id"))
  private Set<Job> jobs = new HashSet<>();
}
@Entity class Job {
  @ManyToMany(mappedBy = "jobs")
  private Set<Person> applicants = new HashSet<>();
}
```

`Person` is the owning side (it defines the join table); `mappedBy` marks the inverse side. Use `Set`, not `List`, to avoid delete-and-reinsert behaviour. Keep both sides in sync with helper methods. Avoid `CascadeType.REMOVE` on many-to-many, since deleting a person must not delete jobs. Collections are lazy by default; use `JOIN FETCH` or an entity graph to avoid N+1.

**Layers.** Controller, service, repository (Spring Data JPA); DTOs rather than exposing entities; `@Transactional` on the apply operation.

**Extras.** Indexes on the join table columns, pagination for applicants, and a `JobSearchStrategy` if search varies.

**Ops questions.** A Dockerfile for the Spring Boot jar, and in Kubernetes a Deployment, Service, ConfigMap/Secret and readiness/liveness probes.
